{{also|Fliegen}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|fliogan|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*fleuganą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*pleuk-}}. Compare Dutch {{term|vliegen|lang=nl}}, Low German {{term|flegen|lang=de}}, English {{term|fly|lang=en}}, Danish {{term|flyve|lang=da}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈfliːɡŋ̩/|/ˈfliːɡən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-fliegen.ogg|audio (Germany)}}
* {{audio|De-at-fliegen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=2|fliegt|flog|geflogen|past subjunctive=flöge|auxiliary=both}}

# {{context|intransitive|auxiliary sein|lang=de}} to [[fly]]; to [[travel]] by [[air]]
#* '''2010''', ''{{w|Der Spiegel}}'', issue [http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/index-2010-52.html 52/2010], page 16:
#*: Passagiere, die aus den USA nach Europa '''fliegen''' und dort umsteigen, sollen ab dem 1. April im Transitbereich nicht mehr kontrolliert werden.
#*:: It is planned that passengers who fly from the United States to Europe and change planes there are not checked in the transit area anymore after April 1.
# {{context|intransitive|figuratively|lang=de|auxiliary: “sein”|lang=de}} to [[rush]]; to [[fly]]; to go quickly
# {{context|transitive|auxiliary haben|lang=de}} to [[fly]]; to [[pilot]]
# {{context|transitive|auxiliary haben|lang=de}} to [[transport]] by air
# {{context|intransitive|colloquial|lang=de|auxiliary sein|lang=de}} to [[get the axe]], to get [[kicked out]]

